# VeriPay — Live Demo Presentation Script
### "I'm going to upload an invoice now and walk you through every card the system produces."

---

## STEP 0 — Upload & Trigger Analysis

> *Upload the invoice, hit Analyse, wait for the spinner to finish.*

**What to say:**
"So what just happened — the moment I hit analyse, the backend spun up about 8 independent modules all running in parallel. Forensics, AI text detection, vendor verification, rule checks — all at the same time. Everything gets bundled into a single result object and the frontend picks it apart card by card. Let me walk you through each one."

---

---

## CARD 1 — Detected Signals
*"The first thing you see is this — a bird's eye view of everything the system found."*

**What it is:**
This card is the master summary. Before you read a single number, you can look at the invoice image itself and see exactly *where* the system thinks something is wrong — highlighted with coloured boxes.

**The fields:**
- **Total signal count** — how many suspicious things were detected across the whole document
- **Spatial findings** — signals tied to a specific region on the page (you can see the box on the image)
- **Document findings** — signals that apply to the whole document, not one spot

**The image overlay:**
- 🔴 Red box = manipulation detected there
- 🟡 Amber box = AI-generated content in that region
- 🔵 Blue box = forensic anomaly (pixel/compression issue)
- 🪸 Coral = a rules violation in that area

**How we got here:**
Every sub-module — forensics, AI, rules — emits a signal with a bounding box when it finds something. The backend collects all of them and draws those boxes on the invoice preview. So this card is literally the union of everything else on the page, shown visually.

---

## CARD 2 — Fraud Risk Assessment
*"This is the headline number — the one score that matters most."*

**What it is:**
The overall fraud probability, shown as a circular gauge. This isn't just one model's output — it's a weighted combination of every module below.

**The fields:**
- **Fraud risk gauge** — the big circular dial, 0 to 100%
- **Risk level pill** — Low / Medium / High / Critical
- **Amplifiers applied** — if multiple *independent* modules all flagged something, the system applies a multiplier. It's saying "these signals aren't correlated, so this isn't noise — something is genuinely wrong"
- **Explanation** — a plain English paragraph generated by an LLM explaining *why* the score is what it is
- **Score breakdown (expandable)** — each component's individual score, its weight, and how much it contributed to the final number

**Thresholds:**
- 0–39% → Low (green)
- 40–69% → Medium (amber)
- 70–89% → High (red)
- 90–100% → Critical (dark red)

**How we got here:**
Each of the 8 modules below produces a score between 0 and 1. The ensemble model applies learned weights to those scores — forensics might be weighted more heavily than AI text, for example. Then if multiple modules independently spike high, amplifiers kick in and push the score up further. Finally an LLM reads all of it and writes the explanation text.

---

## CARD 3 — Ensemble Fraud Score
*"This is the technical breakdown of what just fed into that gauge."*

**What it is:**
The raw numbers behind Card 2. Where Card 2 gives you the final weighted score, this card shows you each input before weighting.

**The fields:**
- **Fraud score bar** — the ensemble model's output (same number as Card 2, just shown as a bar)
- **Score breakdown** — expandable list of each sub-module's raw score with a mini bar chart
- **Model version** — which version of the ensemble model was used (important for reproducibility)

**How we got here:**
After all modules run, the ensemble model (a weighted linear combiner trained on labelled invoice data) takes every score as input and outputs one number. We store the per-component scores so analysts can see exactly which module drove the result.

