## CARD 4 — Forensic Analysis
*"This module looks at the invoice as an image and asks — has this been touched after it was created?"*

**What it is:**
Pure image forensics. No AI text, no bank lookups — just pixel-level analysis of the document.

**The fields (always shown):**
- **ELA score** — Error Level Analysis. When you save a JPEG, compression leaves a uniform "fingerprint." If someone pastes in a region, that region has a *different* compression level. ELA detects that mismatch.
- **Font inconsistency score** — scans every text block and checks if the font matches the rest of the document. A pasted total in Arial when everything else is Times New Roman? Flagged.
- **Noise inconsistency score** — natural documents have consistent noise. If a region was spliced in from another image, its noise profile won't match.
- **Text rendering score** — checks for text that was rendered differently (e.g. typed over vs. printed)
- **Metadata anomaly score** — the PDF/image metadata says it was created in Word, but the visual content looks like it was produced in Photoshop. Mismatch = flag.
- **"Triggered" badge** — shown next to any layer that crossed its threshold

**Advanced layers (expandable):**
- **DCT artifacts** — JPEG stores data in 8×8 blocks. Editing and re-saving creates "ghost" block boundaries. We detect those.
- **Copy-move forgery** — detects if a region of the *same* document was duplicated elsewhere (e.g. copying a legitimate line item and pasting it with a modified amount)
- **Input quality score** — how good was the image quality? Low quality = less reliable forensics, so we report it.

**How we got here:**
The invoice PDF is rasterised to an image. All layers run in parallel on that image. Each layer has a threshold — if crossed, it emits a "triggered" signal that bubbles up to Card 1.

